With YOYO_ASK_OUTSIDE_READS=1 set, a search or list_files call with no root is now checked as the current directory. So /cd ~ followed by a search with no root now asks before walking ~. This is commit 2c31d7ee, and fmt, clippy and the full cargo test all pass. The commit is local; the push and the #879 comment are done by me and the harness, and the comment already names the commit.

What yoagent 0.25.7 does with no root (read from its source):
• SearchTool reads path, then falls back to its own root, then to ".". yoyo builds it with SearchTool::default(), so that root is empty and it ends up at ".".
• ListFilesTool reads path and falls back to ".".

Changes:
• `src/outside_read.rs`: a new pure function effective_root(tool, path) turns a missing root into "." for those two tools only. The existing decision code then resolves it against the current directory at call time, exactly like an explicit .. If the current directory can't be read, the existing no_cwd_refusal refuses the call, so there is no second policy. read_file with no path is still not checked, since the tool just reports the missing argument. check now takes Option<&str>.
• `src/tool_wrappers.rs`: the old code only called the check inside if let Some(path), so a call with no root never reached it. Fixing that needed an edit here: I moved the call to just after that block in both wrappers. The file is still 5692 lines, so tests/module_size.rs needed no change.
  • One side effect: for read_file, the pipe/device-file refusal now runs before the outside-read prompt. That check only looks at file metadata, not contents.
• Docs: the "known gap, #879" sentence in permissions.md and the matching module doc comment now describe the new behaviour.

Tests:
• A search or listing with no root, after a /cd outside the launch directory, is refused when there's no terminal and asked once (as a recursive check of that directory) when there is one.
• The common case, where the current directory is still the launch directory, counts as inside and asks nothing (assert_eq).
• An explicit root inside the launch directory, used from a moved directory, is unchanged.
• An empty root gives the same result as no root.
• An unreadable current directory gets the existing refusal.

Positive control: I made the missing-root case return "nothing to check", ran the tests, and restored the code in one command, one control at a time. unrooted_search_after_cd_is_checked_as_the_cwd and unrooted_default_edges went red by name, the common-case test stayed green, and all were green again after the restore. One limit: this control only tests effective_root. No test covers the moved call in tool_wrappers.rs.

Still open on #879: the fence is still opt-in, and bash can still cat files outside the tree.
